This study examines rapid neutrino-mediated processes in detailed 3D core-collapse simulations, focusing on the evolution of energy instabilities for both electron-type antineutrinos ($ ar{
u}_e$) and electron-type neutrinos ($
u_e$). The analysis highlights that while these two species exhibit similar underlying instability mechanisms, their evolution diverges significantly due to the lack of off-diagonal flavor-flavor opacities included in the $ ar{
u}_e$ diagonal opacity term. Consequently, this research provides the initial demonstration of how rapid neutrino type instabilities behave in fully resolved 3D environments.

The paper examines a Steklov-Robin characteristic value problem defined on the unit disk $D=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^2:x^2+y^2<1\}$. The problem seeks a characteristic value $\lambda$ and a corresponding eigenfunction $u:D\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
\begin{equation}\label{eq1}
\begin{split}
\Delta u + \lambda u = 0 \quad \text{in } D, \\
u_r + \lambda^{\frac{1}{2}} u = 1 \quad \text{on } \partial D,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $u_r$ represents the radial derivative at the boundary. We demonstrate that there exists a countably infinite set of real characteristic values $\{\lambda_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$ which diverge to $+\infty$ as $n\rightarrow\infty$, with each associated eigenfunction exhibiting a specific nodal structure.
